  "summary": "The regulatory and innovation landscape saw significant movement this week across vaccines, oncology, and healthcare operations. The FDA approved Moderna's mRNA flu vaccine mFLUSIVA and Replimune's melanoma treatment after previous rejections, marking wins for both companies despite some ongoing uncertainties with the CDC and in regulatory processes. Meanwhile, concerns about Medicare decision-making surfaced in reporting on a seven-year delay in price negotiations for an AbbVie drug and limited patient awareness of the ACCESS chronic care pilot program. On the technology front, health systems are increasingly adopting AI-powered tools to reduce clinician burden through ambient scribing and clinical decision support, while also taking cautious approaches to broader AI adoption, and healthcare organizations are experimenting with new delivery models including drone delivery and tele-ICU services. The week also touched on broader policy questions, including a Senate move to hold Anthony Fauci in contempt over his Covid role and an opinion calling for a 9/11 Commission-style investigation into the U.S. pandemic response.",
